Mohan,
	Here's what's happening. 

You are telling Linux to mount the root partition on "root=/dev/sda2".

Linux will allocate drive names as it sees drives.

So, when the drives are Inserted, the linux layout looks like the following:

> [Tru64 Unix BOOT DISK]  DKA0       RZ2DA-LA N1H1      sda
> [Tru64 Unix]            DKA100     RZ2DA-LA  N1H1     sdb
> [CDROM]                 DKA500     RRD47  1206
> [LINUX]                 DKB0       RZ2DD-LS  0306     sdc
> [Tru64 Unix]            DKB100     RZ1CB-CS  0844     sdd

However, when you pop them out, it looks like this:

> [Tru64 Unix BOOT DISK]  DKA0       RZ2DA-LA N1H1      
> [Tru64 Unix]            DKA100     RZ2DA-LA  N1H1     
> [CDROM]                 DKA500     RRD47  1206
> [LINUX]                 DKB0       RZ2DD-LS  0306     sda
> [Tru64 Unix]            DKB100     RZ1CB-CS  0844     


When the drives are in, Linux is getting confused becuase you are
telling it mount a linux filesystem from a Tru64 partition. (sda)

When they're out, it works fine. 

Change the root partition from root=/dev/sda2 to root=/dev/sdc2, and it
should work. 

(Use show os_boot* at the SRM to find the appropriate flag)

--Phil

