> If compaq really wants to win over linux users, they should add high
> quality optimization for alpha directly into gcc.  Intel is already
> doing this for their cpu's.  I'd hate to see compaq/alpha squander the
> potential gains it could make in linux mind share by not fully
> supporting open source tools.  (I do appreciate their releasing
> free-as-in-beer compilers!  It just seems that there is a lot of code
> duplication in maintaining separate compilers rather than beefing up the
> alpha code generation in gcc.)
> 

Well gcc does have some problems that are hard to overcome.  I believe at 
least some people from compaq have been helping with gcc, but I think a better 
solution is to have the gcc front end hooked up to the GEM backend(GEM is 
actually the optimization core of all the compaq compilers.  Basically all the 
compilers for WinNT, VMS, and Tru64 represent an intermediate format to GEM 
which does the optimization and finalization).  

Part of the problem is that gcc will always be behind the vendor compilers 
because it just has different goals.  the Intel reference compiler, Compaq's 
GEM, etc, don't have the overhead of being fully portable compilers.  

Also I would be interested in what high quality optimization intel is doing 
for GCC.  I believe that if you compare irc to gcc you will see a large 
difference as well.


Aaron Spink
