As long as both fans are running and not obstructed by cables, etc, you
should be fine.  These systems are spec'd to run at 35C ambient.  That =
means
that we test them in a thermal chamber at a significantly higher =
temperature
(42? 45?) as well as testing them in the corners of the
temperature/voltage/frequency graph.

On the other hand, if the reason things are hot is that you've run your =
SCSI
cable across the mouth of the fan, then you should move it. :-)

As far as the discussion concerning heat and electrical consumption =
goes
(but remember students, heat !=3D temperature), the power supplies used =
in the
CH320 are 70-80% efficient.  That means that a system that wants 400W =
will
have already dissipated 133W (400*(.75/1-1)) in the power supply before
anything gets to the motherboard.

It's 3.42 BTU per watt and 12,000 BTU per ton of A/C.  Depending on =
your
configuration and how hard you are running them, you'll need an =
additional
ton of cooling for each 4 to 12 or more systems depending on whether =
you are
running your dual 1 GHz maxed configurations flat out all the time or =
have
uniprocessor 667 MHz systems just loafing along.

Tom

P.S.  Like many systems these cool front to back.  So line them up in =
racks
with the fronts of adjacent rows facing each other and the backs facing =
the
backs of the row behind.  That way you aren't blowing hot outlet air =
into
the intakes of the next row.

